<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Ai</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            Agent decision-making: a compiled blackboard, one action surface that a behaviour tree, a
            utility set and a GOAP plan all choose from, per-agent memory that is a byte range rather
            than an object, and a governor that decides who gets to think this frame. A planner and a
            perception model, not a behaviour library.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>ai;behaviour-tree;utility;goap;blackboard;agents;game-engine;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            ⚠ The reference list is the point of this assembly, not an accident of it. docs/plan/37
            § "Why this is Core and not Gameplay": a behaviour tree that depends on a combat package
            is a behaviour tree that cannot run a stealth patrol, a shopkeeper or a formation of
            ships. Nothing here may reference the gameplay spine, and the `Core/` ⇸ `Gameplay/` line
            in Build.ArchitectureRules.cs is what refuses it — so a violation stops compiling rather
            than failing a test. `AiReferenceAllowlistTests` is the other half and the stricter one:
            these six by name, so that a new reference is a decision somebody makes.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core\Vixen.Core.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Mathematics\Vixen.Core.Mathematics.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Threading\Vixen.Core.Threading.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Ecs\Vixen.Ecs.csproj" />

        <!--
            A `.vxbt` is an artefact: a content build writes it and a player reads it back, so both
            halves need the generated serializer and the descriptor over the same types. Same
            arrangement Vixen.Animation uses for MoveSetContent, and for the same reason — the type is
            a runtime assembly's, because a build deserialises it with no importer linked in at all.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Serialization\Vixen.Core.Serialization.csproj" />

        <!--
            Not Vixen.Engine, yet. Doc 37 names it in the reference set because P4's movement,
            rotation and animation tasks need LocalTransform and a scene — and those live in
            Vixen.Ai.Nodes, which is a separate assembly precisely so that this one keeps a narrow
            surface. A reference nothing uses is a reference nobody can remove later.
        -->
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Serialization.Generator\Vixen.Core.Serialization.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />

        <!--
            And the descriptor over the same types. The serializer writes the bytes; this is what
            makes the *alias* resolvable, which is what an artefact's type string is looked up
            through at load. Without it the string resolves to nothing and the pipeline falls back to
            an editor type — a build that succeeds and a game that throws about content it just made.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Reflection.Generator\Vixen.Core.Reflection.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Ai.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
